\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A shared spatial edge and two time edges}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On the #{4\times4} grid, the closed time halves are
#{\{0,1,2\}} and #{\{2,3,0\}} on each time circle. A
spatial link at time zero and its inverse can be read in
either [connection half-algebra](fdip-019L). The time link
from zero to one is in the positive half, and its image
under #{\theta_s} goes from zero to three in the negative
half. Its value there uses the inverse of the positive
time link from three to zero. Inversion of both coordinates
also sends the first time edge into the negative half.
Spatial inversion alone keeps the time corners of an edge
in their original half.}
